{:edb/db-before-t 1271 :edb/db-after-t 1272 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534584 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:38:33.145Z" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078279 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078279 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4}}$." 13194139534584 false] [17592186078279 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to 4^{+}}\\frac{x - 2\\sqrt{x}}{x - 4} &= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{4}}{4 - 4} = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\\ &=\\dfrac 1 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 false] [17592186078279 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{4-2\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n            &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\n\t    &=\\dfrac 1 2.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078280 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078280 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}}$." 13194139534584 false] [17592186078280 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} &= \\frac{3 \\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{0 + 1} - 1} = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(3x)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2-1^2} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x+1-1} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}3(\\sqrt{x+1}+1) \\\\ &=3(\\sqrt{0+1}+1) \\\\ &=6. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 false] [17592186078280 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} \n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(3x)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2-1^2}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x+1-1}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}3(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)\\\n&=3(\\sqrt{0+1}+1)\\\n\t&=6.\n}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297004 13194139534584 true] [17592186078455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297008 13194139534584 true] [17592186078455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297023 13194139534584 true] [17592186078476 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297012 13194139534584 true] [17592186078476 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297016 13194139534584 true] [17592186078476 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297030 13194139534584 true] [17592186078841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534584 true] [17592186078841 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x + 4} - 2}{x}$." 13194139534584 false] [17592186078841 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078841 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078842 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1^{-}}\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x + 3} - 2}$." 13194139534584 false] [17592186078842 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2}.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186078842 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{1+3}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+3}-2)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x+3-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\sqrt{x+3}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3}+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186264521 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297015 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297005 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1089 "q-338481" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1113 #uuid "92252b75-35d4-5466-b5eb-58457897a6af" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297004 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to }\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8(8)}}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{(8-\\sqrt{8x})(8+\\sqrt{8x})}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{8}^{2}-(\\sqrt{8x})^{2}}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{64-8x}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8(x-8)}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8}{8+\\sqrt{8x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+\\sqrt{8(8)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297006 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297007 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1041 "field-1" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297006 13194139534584 true] [17592186297005 1110 #uuid "d2f5daf8-e978-513a-b567-a86a92f7824d" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297006 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297006 1111 #uuid "17b52a57-d327-59a4-87f1-8f2c1f94c3ea" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297007 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297007 1111 #uuid "1fc99c1f-75f5-59ab-9047-139852f97b77" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297009 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1089 "q-338484" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1113 #uuid "6417b838-8f56-5a40-8b5a-45b5e7ecb695" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297008 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297010 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297011 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1041 "field-1" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297011 13194139534584 true] [17592186297009 1110 #uuid "b9ce42d7-ba17-543a-ad4c-ba3d8f394931" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297010 1034 "\\frac{1}{24}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297010 1111 #uuid "333dc7a9-ef74-53f8-ae90-5e5e7294c3b2" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297011 1034 "\\frac{1}{20}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297011 1111 #uuid "adfbf7e5-724e-570d-a920-f0b2140fdbf8" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297013 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1089 "q-338496" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1113 #uuid "cea8ecd3-a2e6-51b4-876e-28c17aeaa239" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297012 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297014 13194139534584 true] [17592186297013 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534584 true] [17592186297013 1041 "field-1" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297013 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297014 13194139534584 true] [17592186297013 1110 #uuid "b39d826d-10a5-561f-a5c0-b456268562a4" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297014 1034 "4" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297014 1111 #uuid "83e713a7-3de6-53c4-b9e2-14eed83e7e63" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297015 1034 "8" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297015 1111 #uuid "df00b693-51c6-5ee8-8e2d-3ad97cd23022" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297017 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1089 "q-4467" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1113 #uuid "4d110047-c802-560a-bccf-6cce5aa93b1f" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297016 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297018 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297019 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297020 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297021 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297022 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1041 "selection" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297018 13194139534584 true] [17592186297017 1110 #uuid "718dc51d-f545-58cd-80c2-cb003c08fff4" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297018 1034 "2\\sqrt{5}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297018 1111 #uuid "7148833d-dcfc-556d-98a2-769bb8ee8426" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297019 1034 "\\sqrt{5}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297019 1111 #uuid "3e41d188-2da4-545b-92dc-0f528eac4be7" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297020 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297020 1111 #uuid "f869a21b-2e40-5816-82a8-baeef2ab7329" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297021 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297021 1111 #uuid "b88d5cc7-80ea-5684-ac5b-b552366580ce" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297022 1034 "0" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297022 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534584 true] [17592186297022 1111 #uuid "23e859d6-7273-5354-bf41-a04a27d7d07f" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297024 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1089 "q-4468" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1113 #uuid "3ff5a4e0-9857-5121-92cf-e9b6328f9ca8" 13194139534584 true] [17592186297023 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{(x-3)(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x+1-4}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(